<h2>What robots.txt controls</h2><p>A robots.txt file gives compatible web crawlers instructions about paths they may or may not crawl. It normally lives at the root of a host, such as <code>https://example.com/robots.txt</code>.</p><h2>How to use this generator</h2><ol><li>Choose the crawler user-agent, or use <code>*</code> for a general rule group.</li><li>Add allow and disallow paths one per line.</li><li>Add your absolute sitemap URL if applicable.</li><li>Review every path before deploying the file at the site root.</li></ol><h2>Robots.txt is not access control</h2><p>A disallow rule does not make a URL private. Sensitive information must be protected with real authentication and authorization. Also remember that blocking crawling and preventing indexing are different concepts.</p><h2>Test carefully before broad blocks</h2><p>A rule such as <code>Disallow: /</code> can prevent a compliant crawler from accessing the whole site for that user-agent. On production sites, review generated rules with someone who understands the site's crawl and indexing requirements.</p>
<?php $toolContent=ob_get_clean();$faqs=[['Where should robots.txt be placed?','It is typically served from the root of the hostname at /robots.txt.'],['Does Disallow make a page private?','No. Robots.txt is a crawler directive, not an access-control system.'],['Can I reference a sitemap in robots.txt?','Yes. You can include an absolute Sitemap directive pointing to the sitemap URL.'],['What does User-agent: * mean?','It defines a rules group intended for crawlers that match the wildcard user-agent, subject to each crawler implementation.']];require __DIR__.'/../includes/tool-template.php';
